    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Tyler Soderstrom** headlining our offensive attack. With a projection of 76.91, he sits comfortably in our \"Strong\" start tier, bolstered by an excellent matchup against McGreevy and a prime #3 hole in the batting order. The 10% park factor bump and the 9% SP skill advantage for our hitters make both **Tyler Soderstrom** and his outfield counterpart **Henry Bolte** (56.72) mandatory starts, despite the latter's minor platoon friction. Joining them in the \"Strong\" category is **Brice Turang** (63.20), whose 8% boost for batting second against Canning, combined with a positive SP skill adjustment, makes him a vital engine for our efficiency ratings today. Conversely, **Seiya Suzuki** faces a daunting challenge against Sale; despite his talent, his score is suppressed to 47.22 by the elite skill of the opposing arm, though he remains in the lineup to avoid wasted game caps in lower-ceiling alternatives.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Cole Young**, who narrowly missed the starting cut with a score of 39.51. The optimizer penalized him heavily for his #9 spot in the order and a negative platoon split against Burrows, keeping him just below our \"Zebras Floor\" of 40.0. While **Vinnie Pasquantino** (57.00) also faces a significant 15% penalty due to his platoon disadvantage against Kay, his superior base projection and the favorable SP xERA of 5.02 kept him in the UTIL slot over the fringe bench options. We are closely monitoring the risks associated with **James Wood** (52.68); while he holds a high floor due to his top-of-the-order positioning, he is facing a tough 3.01 xERA starter in Burns, making his performance a potential pivot point for our daily Roto output. With **Corbin Carroll** inactive today, we are banking on the high-contact approach of our infielders\u2014**Josh Naylor** (61.99) and **Austin Riley** (49.81)\u2014to provide the necessary stability against their respective matchups.",
